1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,780
GitHub was built for people taking turns.

2
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:10,100
Agents don't. Hundreds of thousands of
them need to change one repo at once.

3
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:13,920
So Forkyard gives every agent its own fork.

4
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:21,120
Each agent works in its own fork, at
once. Every task is its own commit.

5
00:00:21,550 --> 00:00:25,670
A live run: 6 agents, 43 commits.

6
00:00:25,700 --> 00:00:33,540
A merge queue lands them one at a time. A conflict
is data, and the agent that caused it fixes it.

7
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:38,680
Now scale it. Drag the crew size from 6 to a hundred.

8
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:45,280
Every agent still gets its own fork.
Nobody shares a branch, so nobody waits.

9
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:48,990
A queue lands changes one at a time.

10
00:00:48,990 --> 00:00:56,120
Instead, crews of ten merge in parallel into
crew lines, and the crew lines merge into main.

11
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:59,840
Conflicts go back to the agent that caused them.

12
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:10,560
Five levels of ten reach a hundred thousand agents.
That is the design. What we ran is a hundred.

13
00:01:10,900 --> 00:01:15,820
Can a human watch a hundred
agents? No. You watch exceptions.

14
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:22,280
The Inspector checks every delivery. Most are
approved automatically. A few are flagged for you.

15
00:01:22,600 --> 00:01:30,720
Open a flagged change and see a live before and
after. Approve it, or reject it and it is reverted.

16
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:34,040
You only pay for work that merges.
